WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.109
It takes a tough skin to be a leader of a
Country, even more so of a Country that basically

00:00:05.109 --> 00:00:10.440
did not exist until a few years before you
took power, even more so if that Country is

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:16.690
torn between two cold-warring blocks, locked
in an arm-wrestling contest that will result

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:21.310
in total destruction … and yet you manage
to give the middle-finger to both factions

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:23.369
and do your own thing.

00:00:23.369 --> 00:00:27.260
This is the story of the leader of post-war
Yugoslavia.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:32.739
He kept together a federation made up of six
nationalities for 35 years; he led the non-aligned

00:00:32.739 --> 00:00:37.260
movement during the Cold war years; and he
survived the assassination attempts of the

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:42.399
most dangerous men on the planet, including
Mr Josef Stalin.

00:00:42.399 --> 00:00:48.449
Please welcome today’s protagonist: Josip
Broz, better known as “Tito”, the man

00:00:48.449 --> 00:00:49.449
who could not be killed.

00:00:49.449 --> 00:01:05.030
The Young Sergeant
Josip Broz was born on the 7th of May 1892

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:10.299
in the town of Kumrovec, near Zagreb, modern
day Croatia, into a large peasant family.

00:01:10.299 --> 00:01:14.700
Croatia at that time was part of the Austro-Hungarian
Empire, a patchwork of nationalities in Central

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.049
Europe which had recently expanded into the
Balkans.

00:01:17.049 --> 00:01:23.510
Josip was a perfect example of this multi-nationalism,
being born of a Croat father and a Slovene

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:24.510
mother.

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:27.640
Very little is known about Josip’s parents
and his early schooling.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:33.030
Actually, even his birth date and his real
name are disputed[TA2] . This is the extent

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.799
to which the man’s life was shrouded in
mystery.

00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:41.780
That is why we will skip straight to his teenage
years, and trying to navigate as much as possible

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:44.340
the certain facts of his life.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:49.659
Aged 15, Josip was apprenticed to a locksmith
in 1907 and in 1910 he completed his training

00:01:49.659 --> 00:01:54.009
and started working as an itinerant metal
worker in Austria and Germany.

00:01:54.009 --> 00:01:58.079
It was around this time that he joined the
Social Democratic Party of Croatia-Slavonia

00:01:58.079 --> 00:02:01.420
in Zagreb, his first sign of political engagement.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:06.270
Josip’s life changed for good in 1913, when
he was drafted into the Austro-Hungarian army.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:10.810
He clearly had a talent for soldiering as
he was singled out for non-commissioned officer

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:15.320
training, and by summer 1914 he had become
the youngest Sergeant-Major in that army.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:21.390
Of course, the summer of 1914 was not a good
time to be a soldier – let alone an NCO

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:25.470
– in the Austro-Hungarian army, or any other
army for that matter.

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:31.400
As the first shots of WWI rung out, Josip
and his unit were sent to invade Serbia, facing

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:33.640
an unexpectedly stiff resistance.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:38.290
But Josip was a good squad leader and in early
1915 he was transferred to Austria-Hungary’s

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:42.000
most important front in the East, to face
Russia.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.260
This was an even tougher opponent and Vienna’s
armies did not do well.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:49.540
In general, Josip’s experience with the
Austro-Hungarian Army strengthened his belief

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:55.100
that it was not much of an armed force, ineffective
in a real war, but used merely as an instrument

00:02:55.100 --> 00:03:00.070
of oppression against Slavic nationalities
and the lower echelons of society.

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:04.570
On the 4th of April 1915 Josip’s unit was
overrun by a cavalry charge.

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:07.960
This was the first documented occasion in
which young Sergeant Broz proved that he was

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:10.560
a tough cookie and would not go down easily.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.480
One of the Russian cavalry men charged straight
at him, but Josip, being a good fencer, was

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.940
able to deflect his two-metre lance and fight
him off.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:23.180
Another Czarist soldier, though, attacked
him from behind and pierced him close to the

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:24.180
heart.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:28.720
That could have been the end of this story,
but Josip survived, albeit severely wounded

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:30.310
and was captured by the Russians.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:35.620
Over the following two years of hospitalisation
and captivity, the once social-democrat became

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:41.420
acquainted with a much more radical ideology:
Bolshevism.[TA3]

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:49.280
Becoming a Communist
By 1917 he had been completely bought over

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:54.000
by the majority Communist faction and had
renounced any affiliation with Vienna’s

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.000
armies.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:59.280
In February 1917 the Czar had been ousted
by Kerenskij’s moderate government, which

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:04.290
did not sit well with the Bolsheviks, nor
with Josip, who actively participated in the

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:08.800
July Days demonstrations in Petrograd and
in the October Revolution which eventually

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:10.730
brought Lenin to power.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:14.910
When the new Soviet government signed the
Brest-Litovsk agreement with the Central Powers,

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:19.010
which left Russia out of the war, Josip could
have easily returned home.

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:24.150
But it tells something about the steely character
of the young activist that he decided to join

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:29.470
instead a Red Guard unit in Omsk, Siberia,
to continue the fight in the Russian Civil

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:32.470
War against the reactionary ‘White’ armies.

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:36.280
Following a White counteroffensive Josip fled
to modern day Kyrgyzstan, but was able to

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:41.220
return to Omsk and join the newly formed South
Slav section of the Bolshevik party – whilst

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:42.510
getting married along the way.

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:45.720
Finally, in October 1920 he returned to his
native Croatia.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:49.630
In the meanwhile, the Austro-Hungarian Empire
had dissolved, giving birth to several new

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:54.540
Countries in Central and Eastern Europe, one
of these being the Kingdom of the Serbs, Croats,

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:57.550
and Slovenes – later better known as Yugoslavia.

00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:02.480
Upon returning, Josip had joined straight
away the Communist Party of Yugoslavia (CPY).

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:08.070
He must have jinxed it, because the Yugoslav
government immediately banned all communist

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:09.070
activities.

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:13.570
For the next three years, the CPY went quiet,
and so did Josip, resuming work at a metal

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:14.580
mill near Zagreb.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:19.790
But in 1923 Josip made contact again with
the CPY, which had by now gone underground.

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:23.910
It seems like clandestine activity suited
well the young metal worker, as he thrived

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:25.440
in the communist organisation.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:30.200
He first acted as a party functionary and
trade union organizer in Croatia and Serbia

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:36.840
until 1927, when he was promoted organisational
secretary for the Zagreb CPY committee.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:41.900
His tenure in this office attracted the attention
of Moscow and of the Comintern, the Soviet-sponsored

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:44.070
organization of international communism.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:49.190
The Comintern rewarded him with an appointment
as Zagreb’s political secretary in April

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:50.190
1928.

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:53.870
After the assassination of a Croat member
of parliament in June 1928, Josip took the

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:58.950
occasion to take the CPY out of the shadows,
by staging street demonstrations against the

00:05:58.950 --> 00:05:59.950
authorities.

00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:04.410
But they reacted swiftly: Josip’s apartment
was raided by the police which found explosive

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:09.330
material, proving his adherence to the insurrectionary
hardline of the Comintern.

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:13.820
Broz was arrested in August and sentenced
to a five-year term, but the trial gave him

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:17.720
a stage to voice his convictions and gain
credit with the party authorities.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:22.870
While Josip was in prison, Yugoslavia was
in turmoil, forever torn by the two opposite

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:28.080
tensions of centralization versus autonomy
of its constituent nations and autonomous

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:33.420
provinces; which by the way are Slovenia,
Croatia, Bosnia-Herzegovina, Serbia, Montenegro,

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:36.220
Macedonia, Kosovo and Vojvodina.

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:41.620
During this period the King Alexander I established
a dictatorship to stem the nationalistic aspirations

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:43.419
of non-Serbian populations.

00:06:43.419 --> 00:06:48.120
The regime also cracked down on the Communist
party by ordering the arrest of most of its

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:49.120
leadership.

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:51.370
The cadres of the party needed to be replenished.

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:56.800
That is why, when Broz was released in March
1934, the leader in exile of the CPY, Milan

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:02.350
Gorkić, summoned him to his Vienna headquarters
to give him a position in the party’s Politburo.

00:07:02.350 --> 00:07:07.030
This was Josip Broz taking his political career
to the next level, from local representative

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:08.860
to one of the national leaders.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:13.520
It was also the occasion in which he assumed
his distinctive pseudonym: Tito

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:24.370
Tito Begins
From February 1935 to October 1936, Tito moved

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:29.460
to the Soviet Union to work more closely with
the Comintern and develop ties with Bolshevik

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:32.550
leadership which would prove useful in his
climb to power.

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:38.010
In 1937 and 38, Joseph Stalin launched his
campaign of purges against political and military

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:43.270
leaders in the USSR, which, through the Comintern,
extended to other Communist parties across

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:44.270
Europe.

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:47.669
The CPY was not exempt, and Gorkić, among
others, was executed.

00:07:47.669 --> 00:07:52.460
According to Serbian journalist Pero Simic,
[TA4] Tito actively profited from the Stalinist

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:53.460
repression.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:57.960
He took the occasion to spy or even actively
betray some of his comrades and friends, thus

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:00.490
painting himself in a good light with the
Comintern.

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:05.810
He was rewarded with a mandate to re-build
the CPY’s Politburo with his hand-picked

00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:11.000
lieutenants — among them one Milovan Djilas,
whom we’ll encounter again later.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:16.430
And of course, the natural next step was for
Moscow to appoint him new secretary-general

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:17.430
of the CPY.

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:22.920
By 1939, Tito’s grip over Yugoslavian Communist
was undisputed and he started laying plans

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:24.070
for the future of the Country.

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:30.150
At a clandestine conference in Zagreb in October
1940 Tito revealed his strategy: first, leverage

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:35.139
the 24000 members of the party to launch an
armed insurrection against Alexander I’s

00:08:35.139 --> 00:08:40.740
reign; second, establish a federal state,
USSR-style, to address the ongoing issue of

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:43.219
Yugoslavia’s multiple nationalities.

00:08:43.219 --> 00:08:50.970
Dangerous Times, Dangerous Men
The big occasion for armed insurgency presented

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:53.139
itself in Yugoslavia’s darkest hour.

00:08:53.139 --> 00:08:58.819
In April 1941, the Kingdom was occupied and
partitioned by the Axis powers, mainly Germany

00:08:58.819 --> 00:09:01.660
and Italy, but also Romania and Bulgaria.

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:05.920
The Yugoslavian theatre during WWII was – to
put it mildly – a confusing mess, which

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:09.730
contributed to sowing the seeds of enmity
amongst its building blocks.

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:11.920
But I’ll do my best to explain it.

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:16.529
After the occupation, the main resistance
force were the Serbian Chetniks of Dragoljub

00:09:16.529 --> 00:09:20.910
Mihailović, loyal to the government in exile
and supported by the Allies.

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:25.360
The CPY had organised its own parallel Partisan
network, led by Tito, with the multiple agenda

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:30.970
of kicking out the Axis, defeating the Chetniks
and taking over Yugoslavia to install a Communist

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:31.970
regime.

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:36.170
Other factions, on the other hand, had started
collaborating with the Axis, mainly the Croatian

00:09:36.170 --> 00:09:38.509
fascist party, or Ustasha.

00:09:38.509 --> 00:09:43.740
In 1942, Italian General Mario Roatta, also
known as “The Black Beast” was charged

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:45.879
with quashing the Communist insurgents.

00:09:45.879 --> 00:09:50.779
To this end he sought – and obtained – an
alliance with the Chetniks, ideologically

00:09:50.779 --> 00:09:52.529
opposed to Tito’s men.

00:09:52.529 --> 00:09:57.139
Roatta’s Italian 2nd Army and his Chetniks
auxiliaries launched a violent repression

00:09:57.139 --> 00:10:02.610
not only against the Partisans, but also civilian
populations in Slovenia and Croatia, especially

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:04.149
in areas bordering Italy.

00:10:04.149 --> 00:10:09.570
An interesting aside: while Roatta was a total
dick to Yugoslavs, he was actively helping

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:15.040
Jews escape from the Ustasha and the Gestapo,
facilitating their relocation to neutral countries.

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:17.029
But the result of the Chetnik’s changing
sides?

00:10:17.029 --> 00:10:21.939
The Allies shifted their support from Mihailovic
to Tito, who was able to increase the scale

00:10:21.939 --> 00:10:23.110
of his operations.

00:10:23.110 --> 00:10:26.890
Leading a successful guerrilla campaign, the
Communist leader was able to control most

00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:30.519
of Bosnia by November 1943, even establishing
an independent Government.

00:10:30.519 --> 00:10:32.929
[TA5]
Tito had by now become such a nuisance to

00:10:32.929 --> 00:10:35.560
the Axis that Hitler took direct interest.

00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:40.100
He charged his most effective henchman with
the secret mission to locate and assassinate

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:41.910
Tito and the Partisan leadership.

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:46.249
This henchman was legendary SS Commando Otto
Skorzeny, already known by then as

00:10:46.249 --> 00:10:47.910
‘the Most Dangerous Man in Europe’.

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:53.420
In February 1944 Skorzeny launched a two-pronged
offensive: ground forces were to surround

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:57.079
the identified location of Tito’s headquarters,
a cave on the Bosnian mountains.

00:10:57.079 --> 00:11:01.939
While the regular troops would distract the
Partisan forces, Skorzeny would attack the

00:11:01.939 --> 00:11:06.499
headquarters with his signature move, a combination
of gliders and paratroopers.

00:11:06.499 --> 00:11:11.149
But the Germans had underestimated the CPY
fighters: they put up a stiff and prolonged

00:11:11.149 --> 00:11:15.550
resistance, pushing back both prongs of the
attack and allowing Tito to escape to safety.

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:17.519
[TA6]
Both the Black Beast and the Most Dangerous

00:11:17.519 --> 00:11:20.790
Man in Europe had failed to catch or kill
Tito.

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:25.179
The government in exile by now had to admit
that they had to negotiate with this man.

00:11:25.179 --> 00:11:30.269
In June 1944 the royal Prime Minister in exile,
Ivan Šubašić, met with Tito and agreed

00:11:30.269 --> 00:11:33.600
to coordinate their activities to complete
the liberation of the Country.

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:37.949
But any hopes of restoring the monarchy were
quashed by the Soviet army: aided by Tito’s

00:11:37.949 --> 00:11:43.070
Partisans, in October 1944 they liberated
Serbia, the last monarchist stronghold.

00:11:43.070 --> 00:11:48.079
As Germany retreated and the Fascist state
in Northern Italy collapsed in April 1945,

00:11:48.079 --> 00:11:53.079
Tito’s Communist forces extended their control
of the whole of Yugoslavia, achieving total

00:11:53.079 --> 00:11:55.059
victory by May 1945.

00:11:55.059 --> 00:11:58.019
But Tito’s Partisans did not stop there.

00:11:58.019 --> 00:12:02.620
Taking advantage of the Axis’ powers collapse,
they extended the national frontiers by taking

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:07.449
the Istrian peninsula and portions of the
Julian Alps, territories disputed with Italy

00:12:07.449 --> 00:12:08.790
since the end of WWI.

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:13.959
The occupation gave way to brutal reprisals
which struck retreating Axis soldiers, Croat

00:12:13.959 --> 00:12:16.889
and Slovene collaborationist, as well as Italian
civilians.

00:12:16.889 --> 00:12:22.700
It is estimated that up to 15,000 were killed,
many thrown still alive into the natural wells

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:30.047
typical of the region, known as ‘foibas’.[TA7]
A Tale of Two Islands

00:12:30.047 --> 00:12:34.059
As the War ended, Yugoslavia was still a Monarchy,
albeit with Tito as Prime Minister.

00:12:34.059 --> 00:12:38.339
But after a major election victory in November
1945 – 80% of the vote!

00:12:38.339 --> 00:12:43.079
– Tito took full control of the Country,
exiled King Peter II and declared the Republic

00:12:43.079 --> 00:12:46.989
… although it would be fairer to describe
it as a dictatorship.

00:12:46.989 --> 00:12:50.070
His first years in power were particularly
brutal.

00:12:50.070 --> 00:12:55.439
In order to consolidate his power, Tito started
purging in pure Stalin-style.

00:12:55.439 --> 00:12:59.769
Mihajlovic, leader of the Chetniks, and other
opposition leaders were executed.

00:12:59.769 --> 00:13:03.850
The Archbishop of Zagreb and other members
of the Catholic clergy were incarcerated.

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:07.920
Tito then moved to nationalize industry and
undertake a planned economy, a plan which

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:12.179
included forcing small farmers to give up
large portions of their produce to the state.[TA8]

00:13:12.179 --> 00:13:16.819
So far, so good, as Tito was fashioning himself
as a bargain Stalin of the Balkans.

00:13:16.819 --> 00:13:22.769
But Tito and the Big Man in Moscow clashed
when the Yugoslav leader intended “exporting”

00:13:22.769 --> 00:13:27.610
his brand of communism to Albania and Greece,
at a time in which the official Comintern

00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:29.509
line was very cautious.

00:13:29.509 --> 00:13:33.829
In the spring of 1948, Stalin started planning
for a purge of the CPY, but Tito was able

00:13:33.829 --> 00:13:38.329
to maintain control over his party, the army,
and the secret police.

00:13:38.329 --> 00:13:42.779
Over in Moscow Stalin, who would not have
a ‘niet’ for an answer, disowned his old

00:13:42.779 --> 00:13:43.779
comrade.

00:13:43.779 --> 00:13:48.420
He publicly condemned Tito, expelled the CPY
from the Comintern and economically boycotted

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:49.420
Yugoslavia.

00:13:49.420 --> 00:13:53.679
Tito, who was above all a pragmatist, did
not shed tears over the rift with his BFFs

00:13:53.679 --> 00:13:58.779
in Moscow and initiated a gradual shift towards
the West, maintaining nonetheless a political

00:13:58.779 --> 00:14:03.290
doctrine equidistant from both Nato and the
Warsaw Pact.

00:14:03.290 --> 00:14:07.949
Inside his borders, Tito tightened the grip
on power aided by a very effective secret

00:14:07.949 --> 00:14:08.949
police.

00:14:08.949 --> 00:14:14.610
These guys started by cracking down on filo-Stalinist
and later on other dissenters, many of whom

00:14:14.610 --> 00:14:19.489
(up to 600 over the years) would be tortured
to death in Tito’s high security political

00:14:19.489 --> 00:14:23.279
prison, Goli Otok, also known as “Devil’s
Island”.[TA9]

00:14:23.279 --> 00:14:27.690
As it befits such a contradictory character,
Tito also enjoyed the natural beauty of a

00:14:27.690 --> 00:14:31.980
“paradise island”, the isle of Vanga in
the Brijuni archipelago, northern Adriatic.

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:36.410
This island was home to one of his many presidential
residences and the one he used to entertain

00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:41.819
many political leaders, from Fidel Castro
to Queen Elisabeth and Persian Shah Reza Pahlavi.

00:14:41.819 --> 00:14:45.799
But Tito had a soft spot for film stars, too
– and Elizabeth Taylor and Sofia Loren,

00:14:45.799 --> 00:14:49.769
to mention just two, were among the many actresses
to pay regular visits.[TA10]

00:14:49.769 --> 00:14:58.199
A Note to Stalin[TA11]
Before we continue with the story of Tito’s

00:14:58.199 --> 00:15:02.649
rule in Yugoslavia, let me tell you about
some juicy details of the rivalry between

00:15:02.649 --> 00:15:03.720
Tito and Stalin.

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:07.470
As you may have guessed, Tito’s move for
independence from Moscow had not made Stalin

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:08.470
very happy.

00:15:08.470 --> 00:15:09.499
It was not only that.

00:15:09.499 --> 00:15:14.179
It seems like Stalin was also fearful and
even jealous of the Yugoslav leader, which

00:15:14.179 --> 00:15:17.230
made him consider the serious possibility
of having him killed.

00:15:17.230 --> 00:15:19.799
Now, that’s enemy you don’t want to have.

00:15:19.799 --> 00:15:24.660
Otto Skorzeny may have been the Most Dangerous
Man in Europe, but he was the Cookie Monster

00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:26.170
compared to Josef Stalin.

00:15:26.170 --> 00:15:30.069
Soon, Lavrenti Berija, head of the NKVD, was
put on the case.

00:15:30.069 --> 00:15:35.670
Stalin’s spymaster orchestrated no less
than 22 assassination attempts against Tito.

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:40.029
These included increasingly outlandish methods,
ranging from your standard weapons such as

00:15:40.029 --> 00:15:44.629
a rifle, or a bomb, and then going into James
Bond territory.

00:15:44.629 --> 00:15:48.619
One attempt for example involved an ornate
jewellery box, which would release a cloud

00:15:48.619 --> 00:15:50.089
of toxic nerve gas when opened.

00:15:50.089 --> 00:15:51.889
But my personal best involved ‘Max’, real
name Iosif Grigulevich, the Soviet agent responsible

00:15:51.889 --> 00:15:55.179
for the brutal murder of Stalin’s other
big rival, Leon Trostky.

00:15:55.179 --> 00:15:59.899
According to a secret memo, Max was instructed
to infiltrate a diplomatic reception in Belgrade,

00:15:59.899 --> 00:16:03.540
posing as the Costa Rican diplomat, Teodoro
Castro.

00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:07.800
Once there, Max was to release Berija’s
most secret weapon: a lethal plague bacteria.

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:11.680
“The death of Tito and every other person
in the room would be guaranteed,” the memo

00:16:11.680 --> 00:16:12.680
said.

00:16:12.680 --> 00:16:15.339
“Max himself would not know anything about
the nature of the substance.

00:16:15.339 --> 00:16:18.679
To save his life, Max would be immunised against
plague beforehand.”

00:16:18.679 --> 00:16:19.679
Now, that’s what I call real, pure hatred.

00:16:19.679 --> 00:16:22.519
But luckily for Tito, and indeed for the whole
population of Belgrade, Stalin’s death in

00:16:22.519 --> 00:16:27.810
March 1953 meant the hit was called off and
Grigulevich was recalled to Moscow.[TA12]

00:16:27.810 --> 00:16:30.970
Speaking of Stalin’s death … it was a
stroke, right?

00:16:30.970 --> 00:16:36.069
Well, according to Slovenian historian Joze
Pirjavec, there may be something else behind

00:16:36.069 --> 00:16:38.320
it – or rather someone else.

00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:42.269
Pirjavec claims that Stalin may have been
poisoned with potassium cyanide and that the

00:16:42.269 --> 00:16:46.930
poisoning had been ordered by none other than
Tito, in retaliation for the attempted assassinations

00:16:46.930 --> 00:16:47.930
against him.

00:16:47.930 --> 00:16:52.199
If this is true, it would be the fulfilment
of a famous note sent by Tito to Stalin, one

00:16:52.199 --> 00:16:55.429
which may well be the most bad-ass letter
ever written:

00:16:55.429 --> 00:16:57.309
“Stalin:
Stop sending people to kill me!

00:16:57.309 --> 00:17:00.199
We’ve already captured five of them, one
with a bomb and another with a rifle…

00:17:00.199 --> 00:17:03.209
If you don’t stop sending killers, I’ll
send one to Moscow.

00:17:03.209 --> 00:17:11.929
And I won’t have to send another”[TA13]
Leader of the Non-Aligned

00:17:11.929 --> 00:17:12.929
And … let’s get back to politics!

00:17:12.929 --> 00:17:18.420
As he moved Westwards, also Tito’s internal
policy started to thaw towards a more liberal

00:17:18.420 --> 00:17:19.420
stance.

00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:23.209
This was mainly thanks to his deputy and chief
advisor, Milovan Djilas[TA14] , an old chum

00:17:23.209 --> 00:17:26.289
from the early days of the clandestine CPY.

00:17:26.289 --> 00:17:29.919
Together they shaped a type of socialism that
allowed workers to manage industrial enterprises

00:17:29.919 --> 00:17:34.720
and even share profits, which was a pretty
liberal idea by Communist standards.

00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:39.540
Tito also relaxed many of the regime's strict
controls, particularly those affecting small

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:40.540
farmers.

00:17:40.540 --> 00:17:44.399
As a result, Yugoslavia became the most liberal
Communist country of Europe.[TA15]

00:17:44.399 --> 00:17:48.120
The West reacted positively by offering financial
aid and military assistance.

00:17:48.120 --> 00:17:53.700
By 1953, Tito had even signed a mutual defence
pact with Greece and Turkey, which equated

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:56.820
to an informal association with NATO.

00:17:56.820 --> 00:18:02.539
After Stalin’s death in 1953 – by poison
or by stroke - Djilas proposed further reforms

00:18:02.539 --> 00:18:07.120
which would have accelerated the drift towards
the West, most importantly to give up the

00:18:07.120 --> 00:18:08.190
one-party system.

00:18:08.190 --> 00:18:13.850
Tito, however, rejected this proposal in January
1954, which led to a rift with his trusted

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:16.970
vice-president, and his eventual exile.

00:18:16.970 --> 00:18:22.080
Tito preferred, instead, exploring a reconciliation
with the new Soviet leadership under Nikita

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:25.460
Khrushchev, who visited Belgrade in May 1955.

00:18:25.460 --> 00:18:30.580
The two leaders agreed on the Belgrade declaration,
which committed Soviet leaders to equality

00:18:30.580 --> 00:18:36.080
in relations with the other communist countries,
a big step forward in formalising at least

00:18:36.080 --> 00:18:37.490
Yugoslavia’s independence from Moscow.

00:18:37.490 --> 00:18:41.570
But in the following years Khrushchev and
his successor Brezhnev happily wiped their

00:18:41.570 --> 00:18:47.269
backsides with the Belgrade declaration, by
crushing the Hungarian revolution in 1956

00:18:47.269 --> 00:18:48.720
and the Prague Spring in 1968.

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:54.519
In both cases, Moscow blamed Tito for inspiring
these dissident movements, which led again

00:18:54.519 --> 00:18:57.600
to frosty Soviet-Yugoslav relationships.

00:18:57.600 --> 00:19:02.519
Disillusioned with both the West and the Communist
bloc, Tito eventually sought like-minded statesmen

00:19:02.519 --> 00:19:06.990
elsewhere and he found them amongst the leaders
of the developing countries.

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:10.019
Negotiations with Gamal Abdel Nasser of Egypt
and Jawaharlal Nehru of India in June 1956

00:19:10.019 --> 00:19:15.980
led to a closer cooperation among states that
were “non-engaged” in the East-West confrontation.

00:19:15.980 --> 00:19:20.490
From non-engagement Tito developed the concept
of “active nonalignment”—that is, the

00:19:20.490 --> 00:19:24.880
promotion of alternatives to bloc politics,
as opposed to mere neutrality.

00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:29.399
The first meeting of nonaligned states took
place in Belgrade under Tito’s sponsorship

00:19:29.399 --> 00:19:33.100
and guidance in 1961.

00:19:33.100 --> 00:19:40.940
Keeping it together
Since the days of WWII and internal divisions

00:19:40.940 --> 00:19:45.490
exploited by the Axis powers, Tito’s big
preoccupation on the home front was to maintain

00:19:45.490 --> 00:19:47.360
the unity of Yugoslavia.

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:51.289
This was the only way by which the federation
could maintain its independence from Moscow

00:19:51.289 --> 00:19:53.820
and increasingly limited reliance on the West.

00:19:53.820 --> 00:19:56.880
As Tito declared:
“We have spilt an ocean of blood for the

00:19:56.880 --> 00:20:01.090
brotherhood and unity of our peoples and we
shall not allow anyone to touch or destroy

00:20:01.090 --> 00:20:02.090
it from within.

00:20:02.090 --> 00:20:06.980
None of our republics would be anything if
we weren't all together; but we have to create

00:20:06.980 --> 00:20:12.000
our own history - history of United Yugoslavia,
also in the future.”[TA16] [TA17]

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:16.419
But Tito’s concept of internal brotherhood
was to be increasingly threatened by demands

00:20:16.419 --> 00:20:19.980
of de-centralisation in the 1960s and 1970s.

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:24.179
Ironically, it was his ‘big idea’ of workers’
self-management which gradually shifted the

00:20:24.179 --> 00:20:29.000
balance of power away from the central party
authority and from federal government.

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:33.309
The resulting tensions for autonomy, first
in the factories, then in provincial centres,

00:20:33.309 --> 00:20:38.200
opened cracks within the CPY leadership between
1963 and 1972.

00:20:38.200 --> 00:20:42.009
During this period Tito launched a second
wave of purges, first addressing factions

00:20:42.009 --> 00:20:46.929
who wanted to increase centralisation of power
and later Croatian and Serbian liberals who

00:20:46.929 --> 00:20:50.100
were calling for more autonomy in their respective
nations.

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:55.320
Tito’s response to the crises of the 1960s
and early ’70s was to fashion a system of

00:20:55.320 --> 00:21:00.510
“symmetrical federalism,” supposed to
formalize equality among the six republics

00:21:00.510 --> 00:21:04.480
and Serbia’s two autonomous provinces (Kosovo
and the Vojvodina).

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:08.759
Unfortunately this system, while not providing
enough autonomy to the smaller republics,

00:21:08.759 --> 00:21:14.380
also annoyed the two largest, Serbia and Croatia,
that felt they were giving them too much autonomy!

00:21:14.380 --> 00:21:18.909
To put it simply: Tito tried to make everyone
happy … and he managed to piss them all

00:21:18.909 --> 00:21:19.909
off instead.

00:21:19.909 --> 00:21:26.279
Only Tito’s prestige and personal charisma
was now holding together a very fragile federation.

00:21:26.279 --> 00:21:33.200
Death and Legacy[TA18]
Everyone in former Yugoslavia now remembers

00:21:33.200 --> 00:21:35.840
where they were on the 4th of May 1980.

00:21:35.840 --> 00:21:39.850
This was the day in which Tito died of heart
failure, aged 88.

00:21:39.850 --> 00:21:44.330
During his remarkable life the leader always
aimed to find a balance, a third way, oscillating

00:21:44.330 --> 00:21:49.950
between East and West, Communism and Capitalism,
Peace and War, Devil’s Island and his own

00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:51.380
private paradise.

00:21:51.380 --> 00:21:55.960
His toughest balancing act, the one between
centralisation and local autonomy, eventually

00:21:55.960 --> 00:21:57.190
did not survive.

00:21:57.190 --> 00:22:01.070
Eleven years after Tito’s death, the former
federation disintegrated amid a series of

00:22:01.070 --> 00:22:06.389
bloody battles and ethnic hatred reminiscent
of the war already fought on those lands four

00:22:06.389 --> 00:22:07.559
decades earlier.

00:22:07.559 --> 00:22:30.519
But that’s another story, which I’m sure
we’ll tell one day or the other.

